 "r1-programme-brief": "The programme brief is the one-page introduction to Project Beacon. It tells a newcomer what problem Beacon solves, what the three releases deliver, who is involved, how success is measured, what it costs and what could go wrong. You, the Programme Manager, write it in week 1, day 1 and refresh it whenever the numbers change. Dr. Helen Marchetti, the executive sponsor, approves it. Leaders, clinicians, vendors and new team members read it before their first Beacon meeting, and you reuse its wording in the business case, the charter and every status report.",
 "r1-exam-to-project-coverage-map": "The coverage map links every exam objective you must pass to the Beacon document and the session where you practise it: all 26 PMP tasks, the four PL-300 skill areas, the eight CISSP domains and the ITIL Foundation (Version 5) topics. You write it in week 1, day 2 and own it. There is no outside approver, though a study partner or mentor can review it. You use it to see where coverage is thin, to plan revision before each exam gate, and to confirm every objective has been practised before you sit.",
 "r1-business-case": "The business case explains why Kestrel should spend money and people on Beacon. It compares realistic options, states the benefits with baselines, targets and owners, sets out costs and risks, and recommends one option. You write it for the executive sponsor in week 1, day 4. Dr. Helen Marchetti owns it and accepted it with the charter on 2026-09-01 (D-01). The CIO, the Chief Privacy Officer and the CISO review it. It is checked again at every gate, and at closure the programme closure report compares outcomes against it.",
 "r1-programme-charter": "The programme charter formally authorizes Project Beacon. It names the sponsor and the Programme Manager, gives the Programme Manager authority to use Kestrel resources, and fixes the objectives, high-level scope, milestones, budget, governance, privacy and security success criteria, and key risks. You draft it in week 1, day 5 from the business case; Dr. Helen Marchetti signs it (D-01, 2026-09-01). Every later plan, change request and gate decision is tested against it, and it changes only through the change-control board and the sponsor.",
 "r1-scope-statement": "The scope statement defines exactly what Release 1 delivers, what it does not, the constraints and assumptions, and how each deliverable is accepted. You write it in week 2, day 2 from the charter and the requirements register. Dr. Helen Marchetti approves it, and the data owners, Dr. Sarah Whitford and Janelle Cardinal, agree the acceptance criteria. The team builds to it, the change-control board uses it to tell a change from a clarification, and the go-live gate on 2026-10-23 checks against it. With the WBS and WBS dictionary it forms the scope baseline.",
 "r1-requirements-and-traceability-register": "The requirements and traceability register records what the emergency and patient-flow leaders, privacy, security and support need, turns each need into a testable requirement, and traces it back to its source and forward to design, build, test and acceptance. You open it in week 2, day 1 after elicitation and complete the traceability in week 2, day 2. Dr. Sarah Whitford and Janelle Cardinal approve the requirements. Tomás Rivera's team builds and tests from it, and the change-control board uses it to see exactly what a change touches.",
 "r1-work-breakdown-structure": "The work breakdown structure (WBS) breaks Project Beacon into its three releases and then into 15 work packages, each with one owner, a budget, dates and outputs you can test. Its dictionary says what each package contains, so nothing is done twice or missed. You build it in week 2, day 3, before the schedule, because every schedule task must belong to a work package. Dr. Helen Marchetti approves it as part of the scope baseline. Package owners plan from it, finance uses it as the cost structure, and the earned value report rolls up through it.",
 "r1-microsoft-project-schedule": "The Microsoft Project schedule sequences every Beacon task, shows the dependencies and the critical path, and fixes the dates of the three gates. You build it in week 2, day 3 by importing khn_schedule.csv into Microsoft Project, checking the links and saving a baseline. You own and update it. Dr. Helen Marchetti approves the baseline, and a gate date moves only through the change-control board, with the sponsor's decision. The team uses it every week to see what starts next and what has no room to slip, and the earned value report draws planned value from it.",
 "r1-stakeholder-register": "The stakeholder register lists everyone who affects or is affected by Beacon: what they care about, how much power and interest they have, how engaged they are now, how engaged you need them to be, and what you will do about the gap. You build it in week 2, day 4 and review it every two weeks and at each gate. You own it, and the sponsor reviews it. Because it holds frank assessments of named people it is classified Confidential and not circulated. You use it to write the communication plan, choose UAT and pilot testers, and prepare for every change-control decision.",
 "r1-communication-plan": "The communication plan sets out who needs what information about Beacon, when, in what form, through which channel and from whom, and how you will know it worked. You build it in week 2, day 4 straight from the stakeholder register. You own it and the sponsor agrees it. The team, the service desk and each message owner use it every week. Review it at every release, because Release 2 (MFA and new access routes) and Release 3 (downtime procedures) bring new audiences.",
 "r1-raid-log": "The RAID log is Beacon's single register of risks, assumptions, issues and decisions. Each entry has an ID, an owner, a response or action, dates and a status, so nothing important lives only in someone's inbox. You open it in week 2, day 5 and keep it for the whole programme; Releases 2 and 3 add to it. You maintain it, owners update their own entries, and the sponsor and change-control board review it at every meeting. At closure, open risks pass to the residual risk register and the decisions section becomes the record of who decided what.",
 "r1-change-control-process": "The change-control process says how anyone asks to change a Beacon baseline (scope, schedule, cost, data and classification, privacy or security controls, service levels), how the impact is assessed, who decides, and how the decision is recorded and passed on. The Programme Manager writes and runs it. The change-control board, chaired by Marcus Bell (E-002), approves the process and decides changes; the sponsor, Dr. Helen Marchetti (E-001), decides anything that moves a gate or uses the management reserve. You set it up in week 2 with the RAID log and use it in week 7 to decide CR-01. It applies to all three releases, every time a baseline would move.",
