 "r1-quality-plan": "The quality plan defines what good enough to release means for Release 1 and how it is proved: the quality gates for data, model, accuracy, performance, accessibility and security, the source-to-target and reconciliation controls, who tests and who accepts, the test methods and evidence, and the defect thresholds for go-live. The Programme Manager owns it, Tomás Rivera (E-006) and Mei Tanaka (E-013) run the data and model tests, and Janelle Cardinal (E-008) and Dr. Sarah Whitford (E-007) accept the results. You write it in week 3 and record the test results in week 5; the go-live decision on 2026-10-23 rests on it.",
 "r1-threat-model-and-architecture": "The threat model and architecture shows how emergency and bed data travel from the clinical systems to the Power BI service and its users, where the data crosses trust boundaries, what could go wrong at each step (using STRIDE), which controls stop or detect it, and the residual risk with its owner. Daniel Osei (E-004) owns it, Sam Okafor (E-020) and Tomás Rivera (E-006) help build it, Priya Raman (E-003) reviews the privacy side and Janelle Cardinal (E-008) accepts it as business owner. You build it in week 3 (schedule task 7, 2026-09-09 to 2026-09-17). The security acceptance criteria and the RLS test pack then prove it.",
 "r1-security-acceptance-criteria": "Security acceptance criteria turn the privacy impact assessment and the threat model into conditions that someone other than the builder can observe and test before go-live. Each criterion states what must be true, how it is checked, who owns it, and its status at the gate. Daniel Osei (E-004) owns the document, Priya Raman (E-003) owns the privacy criteria, Sam Okafor (E-020) and Tomás Rivera (E-006) run the checks, and the go-live gate on 2026-10-23 decides on them. You write them in week 3 with the threat model and record their status in week 5.",
